    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "It has been observed, in several strike-slip environments worldwide, sets of parallel or sub- parallel faults with very complex pattern and diverse orientations. The block and fault rotation model has contributed to explain the contemporaneous slip of these faults without violating the traditional failure criteria.\r\n\r\nIn the paper we present a succinct chronicle of the evolution of the block rotation theory along with the major challenges found in its development. The second part of the study introduces 2D kinematic and mechanical program developed using Matlab. This developed program has proven to be a useful tool to visualize Block Rotation problems and to help understand the block rotation process in a more comprehensive way.\r\n\r\nThe final part of the paper is devoted to identify the most critical aspect of the block rotation model that requires further research. Among the most important are: the factors that control the spacing of the faults and how the spacing varies after the firsts cycles of rotation and, the role of the rock strength and frictional properties in the rotation of older faults and development of new faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
